You are acting as a senior quantitative model architect and graph analytics engineer.

I already have a deterministic PathWise technical graph extracted from exported model documentation and Data Source schema.

Authoritative inputs:

- nodes.csv
- edges.csv
- optional model documentation / formulas / node metadata

Do NOT rebuild the knowledge graph from raw text.
Treat nodes.csv and edges.csv as the source of truth for technical structure and dependencies.

The goal is to reduce thousands of low-level PathWise objects into a manageable number of candidate methodology groups and architecture components that can support model factorization, reuse analysis, and future model-change impact analysis.

This is inspired by GraphRAG-style hierarchical graph community detection, but the PathWise graph already exists and should be used directly.

==================================================
OBJECTIVE
==================================================

Build a pipeline:

PathWise Nodes + Direct Edges
    ↓
Graph-aware candidate communities
    ↓
Community evidence packages
    ↓
LLM semantic labeling
    ↓
KEEP / MERGE / SPLIT recommendations
    ↓
Candidate Architecture Components
    ↓
Human / SME review

Do NOT assume:

Library = Architecture Component

and do NOT assume:

Graph Community = Architecture Component

A graph community is only a structural candidate.

A final architecture component should represent:

cohesive methodology
+ shared business/model purpose
+ meaningful internal dependency
+ relatively stable interface
+ meaningful change boundary

==================================================
PHASE 1 — LOAD AND CLEAN THE TECHNICAL GRAPH
==================================================

Read:

nodes.csv
edges.csv

Build a directed NetworkX graph.

Use canonical node IDs.

Preserve node metadata such as:

- native_type
- scope
- parent library/group
- Data Source
- input/output status
- Dictionary / Computation / Projection
- UDF
- source location
- formulas / expressions when available

Preserve edge metadata such as:

- relation type
- resolution method
- confidence
- source/evidence
- direct edge only

Exclude or down-weight clearly technical noise where appropriate, such as:

- comments
- literals
- built-in functions
- unresolved placeholders
- synthetic UNKNOWN nodes
- trivial local temporary variables

Do not delete meaningful technical nodes from the source data; instead allow them to receive lower clustering weight.

==================================================
PHASE 2 — CREATE NODE FINGERPRINTS
==================================================

For each node, create a structured fingerprint.

Include:

- node name
- native type
- parent Library / Project Calculation
- scope
- major direct predecessors
- major direct successors
- Data Sources used
- Library Inputs
- Library Outputs
- UDFs called
- whether it maintains state
- whether it uses prev()/last() or equivalent temporal logic
- whether it is a Projection variable
- whether it is an Input / Output / State / Selector
- representative formula or expression when available
- cross-library incoming edges
- cross-library outgoing edges

The fingerprint should summarize what the node does structurally, not invent business meaning.

==================================================
PHASE 3 — GRAPH STRUCTURAL FEATURES
==================================================

Compute useful graph features such as:

- in-degree
- out-degree
- fan-in
- fan-out
- cross-library incoming edges
- cross-library outgoing edges
- boundary/interface score
- parent Library
- shared Data Source usage
- shared downstream consumers
- shared upstream providers
- stateful indicator
- input/output indicator

Do NOT use strongly connected components as the main architecture method.

SCCs may be reported, but this model is primarily a directed dependency/data-flow graph.

Prioritize:

- dependency neighborhoods
- shared interfaces
- shared state
- shared upstream/downstream patterns
- cross-library boundaries

==================================================
PHASE 4 — HIERARCHICAL COMMUNITY DETECTION
==================================================

Use Leiden community detection, preferably hierarchical Leiden if available.

If the current graph must be converted to an undirected weighted graph for Leiden, preserve the original directed graph separately for later interface analysis.

Create edge weights that reflect architectural relevance.

Suggested weighting logic:

Higher weight:
- direct data dependency
- same Library / same Project Calculation
- shared state evolution
- formal Input/Output relationship
- repeated strong methodology relationship

Lower weight:
- generic utility usage
- very common global Data Source dependency
- generic timing/calendar dependency
- common technical helper UDFs
- broad market variables used everywhere

Do not allow high-degree generic nodes such as:
time,
rate,
account_value,
generic market inputs,
common utility UDFs

to dominate community formation.

Produce multiple hierarchy levels if possible.

Example:

Level 0:
small methodology clusters

Level 1:
larger functional groups

Level 2:
candidate architecture domains

Do not automatically treat the highest-level community as the best architecture component.

