==================================================
PHASE 9 — COMPONENT MERGE / SPLIT LOGIC
==================================================

A community should be SPLIT when:

- it clearly contains multiple unrelated business capabilities
- it has weak internal cohesion
- it spans separate stable interfaces
- one part is common utility and another part is business methodology
- different subgroups have distinct change boundaries

Two communities should be MERGED when:

- they jointly implement one methodology
- they share a stable input/output contract
- their separation appears to reflect historical implementation rather than architecture
- model changes normally require them to be considered together

Keep communities separate when:

- they are strongly connected only because of shared generic inputs
- they represent upstream provider vs downstream consumer
- they have distinct business capabilities
- they should be reusable independently

